--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NATIVE ENGINES  (generated from engines.toml)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every one of these ships as a REPLACEABLE SHARED LIBRARY beside its worker
binary. None is statically linked.

Component      Version   Licence                      Worker      Link mode
-------------  --------  ---------------------------  ----------  ----------
libheif        1.23.1    LGPL-3.0                     oc-images   dynamic
libde265       1.1.1     LGPL-3.0                     oc-images   dynamic
libraw         0.22.1    LGPL-2.1-only OR CDDL-1.0    oc-images   dynamic
lcms2          2.19.1    MIT                          oc-images   dynamic
zlib           1.3.1     Zlib                         oc-images   dynamic
libmp3lame     3.100     LGPL-3.0                     oc-audio    dynamic
libopus        1.5.2     BSD-3-Clause                 oc-audio    dynamic
pdfium         7215      Apache-2.0                   oc-pdf      dynamic
onnxruntime    1.20.1    MIT                          oc-ai       dynamic

Upstream sources, one per row, are recorded in engines.toml.

**Link mode is not a formality.** Four of these are LGPL: libheif and libde265
(LGPL-3.0), libraw (LGPL-2.1 / CDDL dual) and LAME (LGPL-2.0+ / LGPL-3.0 as
packaged). The LGPL requires that a user be able to substitute their own build
of the library.

OpenConvert discharges that by linking every LGPL engine DYNAMICALLY and shipping
it as a replaceable .dll / .so / .dylib -- NOT by the subprocess boundary. The
subprocess boundary is real and it is in the wrong place: it sits between
openconvert-run and the worker, while the library sits INSIDE the worker, linked.
The LGPL cares about the boundary between the library and whatever contains it.

For every LGPL component:

  * the full licence text ships in the installed package,
  * the source for the exact version is available at the upstream URL in
    engines.toml and mirrored with each release,
  * the dynamic linkage is verified by a CI gate that fails any LGPL engine
    recorded with link_mode = "static".

**GPL and AGPL are refused outright, whatever the linkage.** No link mode
discharges the GPL for a work we distribute, and offering one as an option
invites someone to choose it. This is not hypothetical: `vcpkg install libheif`
-- the obvious command -- installs x265 (GPL-2.0) by default, because libheif's
default feature set is ["hevc"] and hevc means HEVC *encoding*. Decoding HEIC
needs only libde265. The flagship heic -> jpg conversion needs no GPL code at
all, and the default install brings it anyway. Build with
`vcpkg install libheif[core]` and it never appears.

zlib is listed because it reaches the user's disk, not because anything calls
it: libraw's DLL fails to load without it. A shipped library absent from the
attribution table is an attribution gap however permissive its licence is.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MODELS  (generated from models.toml)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(none -- see models.toml)

The model table is empty and conversion uses no model. The AI pack is a separate
optional download; every model in it is permissively licensed, redistributable,
content-addressed and hash-pinned, and the trust root ships with the app.
Pickle checkpoints are refused at the file-type layer, before anything opens
them, because loading one executes code.

